===Etymology===
From {{etyl|hi|en}} ([[w:Hindustani language|Hindustani]]), from {{etyl|ar|en}} {{m|ar|سائس}}, from {{m|ar|سوس||to tend a horse}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/saɪs/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|es}}

# {{label|en|India}} A [[groom]], or servant with responsibility for the horses.
#* {{quote-book|author=Rudyard Kipling|authorlink=Rudyard Kipling|chapter=Miss Youghal’s Sais|title=Plain Tales from the Hills|location=Calcutta|publisher=Thacker, Spink and Co.; London: W. Thacker & Co.|year=1888|oclc=228690273|passage=Here all trace of him was lost, until a '''''sais''''' or groom met me on the Simla Mall with this extraordinary note.}}
#* '''1890''', [[w:Flora Annie Steel|Flora Annie Webster Steel]]; Grace Gardiner, “The Duties of the Servants”, in ''The Complete Indian Housekeeper and Cook: Giving the Duties of Mistress and Servants, the General Management of the House and Practical Recipes for Cooking in all its Branches'', Edinburgh: F. Murray, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/228145908 228145908]</small>; reprinted Cambridge: {{w|Cambridge University Press}}, 2010, <small>[[Special:BookSources/9781108021937|978-1-108-02193-7]]</small>, [https://books.google.com/books?id=HgzjIUJndx8C&pg=PA68 page 68]:
#*: THE DUTIES OF THE '''''SAIS''''' OR GROOM. {{...}} Now, if the good house-mother's proudest boast is that not even "the cattle within her gate" fail to feel her kindly care, she will often find it necessary to take an active part in teaching the '''''sais''''' his duty, and seeing that the horses receive proper attention. {{...}} The old plan of a '''''sais''''' and a grass-cutter to each horse is a thing of the past, and the number of '''''saises''''' or grooms should have reference merely to the amount of harnessing and out-work necessary during the day.
#* {{quote-book|author=Apa Pant|chapter=Dreams and Destinations|title=A Moment in Time|location=Bombay|publisher=[[w:Orient Blackswan|Orient Longman]]|year=1974|page=76|isbn=837947596|passage=The horse also, as if it had its own premonitions, refused to get on to those rickety planks, though the rest of the party had negotiated them safely. The '''''sais''''' (horse attendant), who had no such anxieties, crossed ahead of us and struggled for some minutes to drag the poor frightened animal, with myself on its back, across the chasm.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Rudyard Kipling|authorlink=Rudyard Kipling|coauthors=James Daley, comp.|chapter=The Maltese Cat|title=Great Horse Stories|location=Mineola, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Dover Publications}}|year=2010|page=95|isbn=978-0-486-47669-8|passage=Each pony had his '''sais''', his groom, who lived and ate and slept with the animal, and had betted a good deal more than he could afford on the result of the game. There was no chance of anything going wrong, but to make sure, each '''sais''' was shampooing the legs of his pony to the last minute. Behind the '''saises''' sat as many of the Skidars'  regiment as had leave to attend the match {{...}}}}

